{"parentUuid":"8b1d3b9d-c0e1-4068-81c6-3468e4abb482","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"11f7002f-1250-416f-bd07-34f6030c1de7","agentId":"a5ed0a16a983cc48c","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01RxRKyThcYDQMfbVwwwwsWq","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"Borradaile Klein planar min-cut max-flow algorithm 2024 implementation\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Maximum Flows and Parametric Shortest Paths in Planar Graphs∗ Jeff Erickson\",\"url\":\"https://jeffe.cs.illinois.edu/pubs/pdf/parshort.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Maximum Flow in Directed Planar Graphs with Vertex Capacities | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-3-642-04128-0_36\"},{\"title\":\"9 An O(n log n) Algorithm for Maximum st-Flow in a Directed Planar Graph\",\"url\":\"https://www.cs.uoi.gr/~loukas/courses/grad/Algorithmic_Graph_Theory/index.files/a9-borradaile.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"A Faster Algorithm for Maximum Flow in 1\",\"url\":\"https://ekfox.web.illinois.edu/publications/vertex_capacities/vertex_capacities.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Multicommodity Max-Flow Min-Cut Theorems and Their ...\",\"url\":\"https://snap.stanford.edu/class/cs224w-readings/leighton99mincut.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"[PDF] An O (n log n) algorithm for maximum st-flow in a directed planar graph | Semantic Scholar\",\"url\":\"https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/An-O-(n-log-n)-algorithm-for-maximum-st-flow-in-a-Borradaile-Klein/21ef60096a44d6b85df98a0dfd753f92b750cda6\"},{\"title\":\"Max-flow min-cut theorem - Wikipedia\",\"url\":\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Max-flow_min-cut_theorem\"},{\"title\":\"Max-flow Min-cut Algorithm | Brilliant Math & Science Wiki\",\"url\":\"https://brilliant.org/wiki/max-flow-min-cut-algorithm/\"},{\"title\":\"Improved Algorithms for Min Cut and Max Flow in Planar ...\",\"url\":\"https://tcs.rwth-aachen.de/lehre/Graphentheorie/SS2018/muensterman_report.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Network Flow (Max Flow, Min Cut) - VisuAlgo\",\"url\":\"https://visualgo.net/en/maxflow\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, I found information about the Borradaile-Klein algorithm, though the results primarily focus on their foundational work rather than recent 2024 implementations. Here's what I found:\n\n## Algorithm Overview\n\nThe Borradaile-Klein algorithm provides an O(n log n) algorithm for finding maximum st-flow in a directed planar graph. After a preprocessing step that consists in finding single-source shortest-path distances in the dual, the algorithm consists of repeatedly saturating the leftmost residual s-to-t path.\n\n## Published Work\n\nBorradaile and Klein published their work \"An O(n logn) algorithm for maximum st-flow in a directed planar graph\" in the Journal of the ACM in 2009. This formulation allows for a simpler presentation and analysis compared to earlier approaches.\n\n## Algorithm Details\n\nThe LEFTMOSTFLOW algorithm finds a maximum st-flow in the graph, and by adding a circulation to the flow, one obtains a maximum st-flow in the original graph.\n\n## Extensions and Improvements\n\nThe search results show that the Borradaile-Klein algorithm has been extended for various applications. Borradaile, Klein, Mozes, Nussbaum, and Wulff-Nilsen developed related work on multiple-source multiple-sink maximum flow in directed planar graphs.\n\nHowever, I did not find specific information about 2024 implementations in the search results. If you're looking for recent implementations or papers from 2024, you may want to check academic repositories like ArXiv, GitHub, or the proceedings of recent conferences (SODA, STOC, ICALP).\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"7e06eb18-75a8-4fa7-9fc6-c99b86bbaf4f","timestamp":"2026-05-07T22:26:16.697Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"8b1d3b9d-c0e1-4068-81c6-3468e4abb482","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd\\.upstream_clones","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"07570229-00ec-4cb0-bebf-74716a2130d6","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"11f7002f-1250-416f-bd07-34f6030c1de7","agentId":"a5ed0a16a983cc48c","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01WBuzmw69udSqyaUVafJo3n","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"grid graph routing disjoint paths algorithm CP-SAT solver planar structure\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"An Approximation Algorithm for the Disjoint Paths Problem in Even-Degree\",\"url\":\"https://www.cs.cornell.edu/home/kleinber/focs05-even.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"The Disjoint Paths Problem: Algorithm and Structure | SpringerLink\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-3-642-19094-0_2\"},{\"title\":\"New Hardness Results for Routing on Disjoint Paths∗ Julia Chuzhoy†\",\"url\":\"https://home.ttic.edu/~cjulia/papers/NDP-hardness-journal.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Improved Approximation Algorithm for Node-Disjoint Paths in Grid\",\"url\":\"https://home.ttic.edu/~cjulia/papers/NDP-in-grids-alg-full.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"THE UNIVERSITY OF CHICAGO ON APPROXIMATING NODE DISJOINT PATHS IN GRIDS\",\"url\":\"https://newtraell.cs.uchicago.edu/files/ms_paper/hongk.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Constant Congestion Routing of Symmetric Demands in ...\",\"url\":\"https://chekuri.cs.illinois.edu/papers/dir-edp-planar-sidma.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Almost Polynomial Hardness of Node-Disjoint Paths in Grids: Theory of Computing: An Open Access Electronic Journal in Theoretical Computer Science\",\"url\":\"https://theoryofcomputing.org/articles/v017a006/\"},{\"title\":\"Approximating disjoint-path problems using packing integer programs\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/226313570_Approximating_disjoint-path_problems_using_packing_integer_programs\"},{\"title\":\"A Faster Algorithm for Finding Disjoint Paths in Grids | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/3-540-46632-0_40\"},{\"title\":\"Rerouting Planar Curves and Disjoint Paths | ACM Transactions on Algorithms\",\"url\":\"https://dl.acm.org/doi/10.1145/3715694\"}]\n\nI found relevant academic research on grid graph routing and disjoint paths algorithms. Here's a summary of what the search results reveal:\n\n## Disjoint Paths Algorithms in Grids\n\nThe disjoint paths problem involves finding vertex- or edge-disjoint paths P₁, P₂, ..., Pₖ in a graph G such that each path Pᵢ joins specified source-destination pairs sᵢ and tᵢ, and this is a central problem in algorithmic graph theory and combinatorial optimization with applications to transportation networks, VLSI layout, and virtual circuit routing.\n\n## Algorithmic Approaches\n\nThe best currently known approximation algorithm for NDP is an elementary O(√n)-approximation greedy algorithm that selects the shortest path connecting any demand pair, adds it to the solution, and deletes all vertices of that path from the graph. However, for grid graphs specifically, the best current upper bound is Õ(n^1/4), with the best lower bound being APX-hardness.\n\n## Grid and Planar Structure\n\nThe brick-wall graph and the grid are structurally very similar, differing primarily in the degrees of their internal nodes (three versus four respectively). Research on disjoint paths in grid-like graphs makes extensive use of structural properties of grids, such as the quadratic growth rate of balls.\n\n**Note:** While the search results contain extensive information about disjoint paths algorithms on grids and planar structures, they do not specifically mention the CP-SAT solver in relation to these routing problems. The results focus primarily on approximation algorithms and complexity theory for these classical problems.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"bb0fe0a4-bc2c-475e-91de-af9b3908447b","timestamp":"2026-05-07T22:26:20.102Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"07570229-00ec-4cb0-bebf-74716a2130d6","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd\\.upstream_clones","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"bb0fe0a4-bc2c-475e-91de-af9b3908447b","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a5ed0a16a983cc48c","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_01RLoArsqoLQh5oHTZF9NMnu","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01MWjLHVy5bqDxHEaLcDjrTC","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd\\src\\models\\routing_subproblem.py","limit":80},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3221,"cache_read_input_tokens":24919,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3221,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":43,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"}},"requestId":"req_011CaozeSRaAXrFYeSjpxtND","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"6c423708-f3cd-4780-943a-42718513c89e","timestamp":"2026-05-07T22:26:23.340Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd\\.upstream_clones","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"6c423708-f3cd-4780-943a-42718513c89e","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"11f7002f-1250-416f-bd07-34f6030c1de7","agentId":"a5ed0a16a983cc48c","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01MWjLHVy5bqDxHEaLcDjrTC","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\"\"\"\n2\tExact grid-routing subproblem.\n3\t\n4\tThis version keeps the exact state semantics, but shrinks the routing core to the\n5\tcommodity-scoped terminal-connected domain before building CP-SAT variables.\n6\t\"\"\"\n7\t\n8\tfrom __future__ import annotations\n9\t\n10\timport time\n11\tfrom collections import defaultdict, deque\n12\tfrom dataclasses import dataclass\n13\tfrom itertools import combinations\n14\tfrom typing import Any, Dict, Iterable, List, Mapping, Optional, Sequence, Set, Tuple\n15\t\n16\tfrom ortools.sat.python import cp_model\n17\t\n18\tfrom src.models.cp_sat_worker_config import (\n19\t    DEFAULT_ROUTING_CP_SAT_WORKERS,\n20\t    resolve_cp_sat_worker_count,\n21\t)\n22\t\n23\tGRID_W = 70\n24\tGRID_H = 70\n25\tDIRECTIONS = [\"N\", \"S\", \"E\", \"W\"]\n26\tDIR_DELTA = {\"N\": (0, 1), \"S\": (0, -1), \"E\": (1, 0), \"W\": (-1, 0)}\n27\tDIR_OPP = {\"N\": \"S\", \"S\": \"N\", \"E\": \"W\", \"W\": \"E\"}\n28\tLAYERS = [0, 1]\n29\tGROUND_LAYER = 0\n30\tELEVATED_LAYER = 1\n31\t\n32\t\n33\tRouteStateKey = Tuple[int, int, int, Tuple[str, ...], Tuple[str, ...], str]\n34\t\n35\t\n36\t@dataclass\n37\tclass RoutingPlacementCore:\n38\t    occupied_cells: Set[Tuple[int, int]]\n39\t    occupied_owner_by_cell: Dict[Tuple[int, int], str]\n40\t    free_cells: Set[Tuple[int, int]]\n41\t    free_neighbors_by_cell: Dict[Tuple[int, int], Tuple[Tuple[int, int], ...]]\n42\t    component_by_cell: Dict[Tuple[int, int], int]\n43\t    cells_by_component: Dict[int, Set[Tuple[int, int]]]\n44\t\n45\t    @classmethod\n46\t    def from_occupied_cells(\n47\t        cls,\n48\t        occupied_cells: Set[Tuple[int, int]],\n49\t        *,\n50\t        occupied_owner_by_cell: Optional[Mapping[Tuple[int, int], str]] = None,\n51\t    ) -> \"RoutingPlacementCore\":\n52\t        occupied = {(int(x), int(y)) for x, y in occupied_cells}\n53\t        owner_map = {\n54\t            (int(cell[0]), int(cell[1])): str(owner)\n55\t            for cell, owner in dict(occupied_owner_by_cell or {}).items()\n56\t        }\n57\t        free_cells: Set[Tuple[int, int]] = set()\n58\t        for x in range(GRID_W):\n59\t            for y in range(GRID_H):\n60\t                if (x, y) not in occupied:\n61\t                    free_cells.add((x, y))\n62\t\n63\t        free_neighbors_by_cell = {\n64\t            cell: tuple(\n65\t                sorted(\n66\t                    neighbor\n67\t                    for neighbor in _cell_neighbors(cell)\n68\t                    if neighbor in free_cells\n69\t                )\n70\t            )\n71\t            for cell in free_cells\n72\t        }\n73\t        component_by_cell, cells_by_component = _compute_free_components(\n74\t            free_cells,\n75\t            free_neighbors_by_cell=free_neighbors_by_cell,\n76\t        )\n77\t        return cls(\n78\t            occupied_cells=occupied,\n79\t            occupied_owner_by_cell=owner_map,\n80\t            free_cells=free_cells,"}]},"uuid":"ce5afb53-fa61-4211-832f-3563f6440908","timestamp":"2026-05-07T22:26:23.351Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"6c423708-f3cd-4780-943a-42718513c89e","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd\\.upstream_clones","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"ce5afb53-fa61-4211-832f-3563f6440908","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a5ed0a16a983cc48c","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_01RLoArsqoLQh5oHTZF9NMnu","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_015VBDTQ63PFvF3HfyexJTSv","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd\\src\\models\\flow_subproblem.py","limit":80},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3221,"cache_read_input_tokens":24919,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3221,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":43,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"}},"requestId":"req_011CaozeSRaAXrFYeSjpxtND","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"7030edb5-592f-49b8-b052-3a12422adeb9","timestamp":"2026-05-07T22:26:24.259Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd\\.upstream_clones","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"7030edb5-592f-49b8-b052-3a12422adeb9","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"11f7002f-1250-416f-bd07-34f6030c1de7","agentId":"a5ed0a16a983cc48c","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_015VBDTQ63PFvF3HfyexJTSv","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\"\"\"\n2\tTopological flow subproblem（拓扑流子问题）.\n3\t\n4\t重要降级说明：\n5\t1. 本模块在 exploratory mode（探索模式）中可作为 accelerator（加速器）使用。\n6\t2. 在 certified_exact mode（严格认证精确模式）中，本模块只允许作为\n7\t   diagnostic（诊断器），不得单独产生正式剪枝证书。\n8\t3. 因此它返回的 INFEASIBLE / UNKNOWN 只能被上层按模式解释，\n9\t   exact 路径不得把这里的失败直接写成 exact-safe cut（精确安全切平面）。\n10\t4. 集成了 Benders Cuts 的提取基础接口。\n11\t\"\"\"\n12\t\n13\tfrom __future__ import annotations\n14\t\n15\tfrom collections import defaultdict\n16\tfrom dataclasses import dataclass, field\n17\tfrom typing import Any, Dict, Iterable, List, Optional, Set, Tuple\n18\t\n19\tfrom ortools.linear_solver import pywraplp\n20\t\n21\tGRID_W = 70\n22\tGRID_H = 70\n23\t\n24\t\n25\t@dataclass\n26\tclass FlowNetwork:\n27\t    \"\"\"Directed flow network（有向流网络） used by the diagnostic LP（诊断线性规划）.\"\"\"\n28\t\n29\t    nodes: Set[str] = field(default_factory=set)\n30\t    edges: Dict[Tuple[str, str], float] = field(default_factory=dict)\n31\t    \n32\t    # Track the origin (machine instance) of port nodes to trace back Min-Cut\n33\t    port_to_instance: Dict[str, str] = field(default_factory=dict)\n34\t\n35\t    def add_node(self, node_id: str) -> None:\n36\t        self.nodes.add(node_id)\n37\t\n38\t    def add_edge(self, u: str, v: str, capacity: float) -> None:\n39\t        self.nodes.add(u)\n40\t        self.nodes.add(v)\n41\t        self.edges[(u, v)] = float(capacity)\n42\t\n43\t    def get_capacity(self, u: str, v: str) -> float:\n44\t        return float(self.edges.get((u, v), 0.0))\n45\t\n46\t\n47\tdef cell_id(x: int, y: int) -> str:\n48\t    return f\"c_{x}_{y}\"\n49\t\n50\t\n51\tdef _front_cell(x: int, y: int, direction: str) -> Optional[Tuple[int, int]]:\n52\t    if direction == \"N\":\n53\t        return (x, y + 1)\n54\t    if direction == \"S\":\n55\t        return (x, y - 1)\n56\t    if direction == \"E\":\n57\t        return (x + 1, y)\n58\t    if direction == \"W\":\n59\t        return (x - 1, y)\n60\t    return None\n61\t\n62\t\n63\tdef build_flow_network(\n64\t    occupied_cells: Set[Tuple[int, int]],\n65\t    port_dict: Dict[str, List[Dict[str, Any]]],\n66\t    commodity_demands: Dict[str, float],\n67\t) -> FlowNetwork:\n68\t    \"\"\"Construct a coarse network（构建粗粒度网络） from occupied cells and ports.\"\"\"\n69\t\n70\t    net = FlowNetwork()\n71\t\n72\t    free_cells: Set[Tuple[int, int]] = set()\n73\t    for x in range(GRID_W):\n74\t        for y in range(GRID_H):\n75\t            if (x, y) not in occupied_cells:\n76\t                free_cells.add((x, y))\n77\t                net.add_node(cell_id(x, y))\n78\t\n79\t    for (x, y) in free_cells:\n80\t        for dx, dy in ((1, 0), (-1, 0), (0, 1), (0, -1)):"}]},"uuid":"31e6c59c-5e92-40d4-a838-8586c8ff701b","timestamp":"2026-05-07T22:26:24.270Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"7030edb5-592f-49b8-b052-3a12422adeb9","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd\\.upstream_clones","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
